--- MESSAGE ---
Source code generation is a transform, and the generator domains exist as structure

Dev's grounding, two days running: Drake is the one definition language; the definition data format
is a configuration parameter of an executable instance, not a structural fact; and generating source
from decoded definitions is cross-domain data transformation like any other, so it gets the domain
and type structure transforms already have. This commit makes that structure exist — as data, with
every member list empty, so the shape is in the tree before any rule is written into it.

THE STRUCTURE

    draco.Generator(T)               the transform from Draco to a target T — a root type
    draco.generator.Generator        the super-domain of every generator transform (carrier under it)
    draco.genscala.GenScala          from draco Generator(ScalaTarget); source Draco; target ScalaTarget
    draco.gendrake.GenDrake          from draco Generator(DrakeTarget);  source Draco; target DrakeTarget
    draco.scalatarget.ScalaTarget    a Target
    draco.draketarget.DrakeTarget    a Target — the emission side of the definition language
    draco.drake.Drake                a Source — the definition language; parse belongs here

Naming settled against the forest scenario, not on it. The forest's Source_Target-under-source rule
degenerates when the source is the root (everything is already under draco), would put two domain
types in one package, and encodes a direction whose inverse can never exist. So each transform is a
peer package named for what it does; the draco/Draco prefixes are dropped where the package already
says it (Dev: GenScala, not DracoGenScala). The one collision that naming had — `draco.Generator` was
the hand-written engine — was resolved at e3c96f8 by renaming the engine to DracoGenerator.

WHAT MOVED, WHAT WENT

`draco.generator.Generator` loses its type parameter and its `generator` function element: the
parameter lives on the root type now, and the super-domain is not a transform. The engine's two faces
move to the transforms that own them — GenScala.generator = DracoGenerator.generate, GenDrake.generator
= Drake.emit — and ScalaTarget and Drake stop carrying them; CLI's `generate` and `drake` commands
point at the new homes. Moved rather than retired, so nothing observable changes until rules replace
them.

WHAT THE STRUCTURE STILL LACKS, DELIBERATELY

The forest showed what a transform needs to run, and none of it is here yet: target-side TYPES for
the morphisms to produce (ScalaTarget and DrakeTarget have no members — the only value anywhere is
String); a target-side RECEIVER, since a fact inserted by a rule gets no working-memory node without a
consumer; an ACTOR per transform to build the knowledge and insert definitions; and PARSE as a member
morphism of Drake, so both legs of the Drake <-> carrier round trip live in the framework. Each is an
increment. GenDrake goes first when the rules come, because both its legs are already gated
(DrakeGenTest, DrakeParseTest) and the rule-driven emitter can be checked byte-for-byte against the
hand-written one it replaces.

TWO PROVISIONAL CHOICES, KEPT

`source` and `target` stay explicit beside `from Generator(X)` although the type argument already
names the target. The presence rule that makes a transform domain a transform domain reads both off
the aspect; changing it for a family of two is premature, and a lint that the argument agrees with
`target` is the fix when there is a third. And it paid for itself immediately: the projection already
imports a transform domain's source, target and super packages, which is what lets
`Generator(ScalaTarget)` resolve across packages with no package spelled inside the parentheses —
type parameters are flat text (#51), and the keyword carries what the parameter cannot. derivationRef
qualified the parent (`extends draco.Generator[ScalaTarget]`) because draco.generator._ is imported
too; that was its job, and it did it unasked.

THE RENAME'S LEFTOVER

The first build failed: \`Generator is already defined as trait Generator\`, in DracoGenerator.scala.
e3c96f8 renamed \`object Generator\` and every \`Generator.\` call site, and its sweep looked for exactly
those — so it missed the engine's own self-description in the same file: an empty \`trait Generator\`,
the \`Type[Generator]\` built on it, an \`apply\` returning it, and a hand-rolled typeDefinition naming
itself "Generator". None of it is referenced outside the file. All of it is DracoGenerator now, so the
engine describes itself by its own name and the definition-backed \`draco.Generator(T)\` has the root to
itself. A sweep that greps for what it expects to find misses what it did not expect; the compiler
found it in three seconds.

A latent hazard, named so it is not rediscovered: any file importing both draco._ and
draco.generator._ (today: Carrier, DefinitionPath) would find a bare `Generator` ambiguous. Neither
names it. A future member of the generator package that does must qualify.

